window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-33"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "Three passes over one buffer, and the constraint is that there is no second buffer. So before anything else I want to agree what in place means here, because it decides whether one helper covers the first two parts or whether I write two loops.", "questions": ["Does in place mean I may not allocate at all, or that I may not hold two full copies of the buffer at once?", "Is a repeated reading a consecutive repeat, or any repeat of that sensor and value anywhere in the buffer?", "Does the partition have to keep samples in their original order inside a band, or only put the bands in order?"], "assumptions": ["In place means one buffer: I may keep a fixed number of cursors and a small map, but not a second copy of the samples.", "A repeated reading is a consecutive one, which is what a sensor stuck on a value actually produces.", "Order inside a band does not matter, which is what lets the partition be a single pass rather than a stable sort.", "Dropping a sample and reporting it are the same event, so the report is built by the pass rather than after it."], "edgeCases": ["an empty buffer and a buffer of one sample, where the cursors never separate", "a buffer where nothing is dropped, so the write cursor tracks the read cursor exactly and every copy is a self-assignment", "a run longer than the buffer's own tail, and a run interrupted and then resumed", "a buffer holding only one severity, where two of the three bands are empty"], "restate": "Rewrite a fixed-capacity telemetry buffer in place three ways: remove the readings flagged invalid, keep at most a given number of samples from each run of identical readings, and reorder the buffer into three severity bands, reporting what happened each time."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One read cursor and one lagging write cursor, which is the same-direction form of two pointers. The first two parts are the same loop with a different keep test, so I write the loop once as a private helper that takes the test and let both public methods be one line each. The third part is the three-region version of the same invariant, and it needs a third cursor coming in from the tail, so it does not share the helper. What it shares is the way of thinking, and I will say that out loud rather than force a call that does not fit.", "dataStructures": ["Two integer cursors and nothing else for the traversal: the whole point is that memory does not grow with the buffer.", "One small dictionary keyed on sensor id for the drop attribution, which is bounded by the number of sensors rather than the number of samples.", "A record type for the report, so the counts and the attribution travel together and the next part does not have to recompute either.", "Three cursors for the partition, and three ranges derived from where two of them stopped."], "types": ["The report is a struct rather than a tuple, because it crosses a part boundary and a named field beats a positional one wherever a caller has to read it.", "The severity is an ordered enum, because the partition is defined by its order and an unordered set of cases would leave that order living in the algorithm instead of the type.", "The partition returns ranges rather than three arrays, which is the honest return for something that reordered a buffer in place: slicing it is the caller's business.", "The error enum carries the offending limit, because a refusal that does not say what it refused makes the caller guess."], "composition": "The keep test is the seam. It is a closure handed the buffer as it stands and both cursors, and that signature is chosen in the first part because the second part needs it: the repeat test has to look at what was already committed, which a test seeing only the sample being read cannot do. The third part does not call the helper and I would rather say why than fake it. Instead the suite asserts the relationship that does hold: partitioning and dropping the invalid readings commute, and partitioning never changes what the buffer holds, only where.", "tradeoff": "Keeping the original order inside each severity band would need either a stable partition with a scratch buffer or three passes, and the single pass forfeits it. I am taking the single pass because the memory budget is the whole premise, and I would rather name what it costs than quietly deliver a sort.", "trap": "Two, and they are different in kind. In the repeat cap, comparing the sample being read against the previous sample read looks right and collapses every run to one; the comparison has to be against what was committed a limit's worth of slots back. In the partition, advancing the read cursor after swapping a sample in from the tail skips a sample nobody has classified, and it is invisible on any buffer whose tail happens to hold the lowest severity."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the private compaction helper first, with the keep test as a parameter, because both of the first two parts are that loop.", "Make the invalid-reading pass a one-line call into it, and check it on a batch where nothing is dropped before checking one where something is.", "Add the trim of the stale tail before moving on, since the report and the buffer disagree without it.", "Write the repeat cap as a second keep test, and say out loud what it compares against before writing the comparison.", "Validate the limit before doing any work, so a bad limit never half-compacts a buffer.", "Write the partition last, starting by naming the three regions and the boundary each cursor holds."], "commentary": ["Everything below the write cursor is final. Everything at or above the read cursor is unexamined. The gap between them is the hole left by drops, and I never sum anything over it, which is what makes this two pointers and not a window.", "I hand the keep test the buffer as it stands, not just the sample being read, because the next part has to look at what I already committed.", "I trim the tail after the sweep. The slots above the write cursor still hold copies of samples I have already moved.", "The repeat test compares against the sample committed a limit's worth of slots back. Comparing against the previous sample read would keep one per run no matter what limit I was given.", "While fewer samples have been committed than the limit allows, everything is kept: there is nothing that far back to compare against yet.", "The keep-or-drop helper has nothing to decide in the partition, so I am not going to force it. Three regions, three cursors, same invariant.", "After a swap with the tail cursor I leave the read cursor alone. What just arrived has not been looked at, and this is the classic place to get it wrong.", "I report every band, empty ranges included, so the caller never has to distinguish an absent key from an empty one."], "checkIn": "Before the third part, say: the partition genuinely cannot use the helper I built, so rather than force a call I would rather write it separately and let the tests assert that the two passes commute. Does that read as the right call, or would you rather I found a way to share the code?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk a batch of three identical readings, one invalid reading, two identical warnings and one critical through the invalid-reading pass: six survive, one goes, and the attribution names the sensor that lost it.", "Walk the same batch through the repeat cap at two: the run of three loses one, the run of two is untouched, and the invalid reading is not this pass's business and stays.", "Walk a run of four identical readings at a limit of two and confirm two survive. This is the shortest fixture that tells the committed-slot comparison apart from the previous-read comparison.", "Walk a batch whose last sample is the lowest severity through the partition and confirm it lands in the first band, which is what a read cursor advancing after a tail swap gets wrong.", "Walk an empty buffer and a buffer of one through all three, where the cursors never separate."], "tests": "Run the compactor's suite and read the three parts in order. The run-of-four fixture in the second suite is the one that separates the two possible comparisons; the interleaved-sensors fixture is what catches a repeat test that matches on the value alone; and the tail-informational fixture in the third suite is what catches a read cursor advancing after a swap from the tail. The commuting test and the contents test in the third suite are the composition, expressed as invariants because a call does not exist here.", "complexity": "Every part is one pass over the buffer, linear in the number of samples, with memory that does not grow with it: a fixed number of cursors plus a drop map bounded by the number of distinct sensors. The partition is a single pass too, not three, which is what the third cursor buys and what the loss of order inside a band costs.", "moreTime": "Making the partition stable, which needs either a scratch buffer or a counting pass first and is a good conversation about what in place is actually worth. A repeat cap that measures a window of time rather than a run of positions, which is where this stops being two pointers and becomes a sliding window. And an uplink budget that drops the least severe samples first once the buffer is still too big after all three passes."}, "code": "public enum Severity: Int, Comparable, Sendable, CaseIterable {\n    case info\n    case warning\n    case critical\n\n    public static func < (lhs: Severity, rhs: Severity) -> Bool {\n        lhs.rawValue < rhs.rawValue\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct Sample: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let sensorID: String\n    public let value: Int\n    public let severity: Severity\n    public let isValid: Bool\n\n    public init(sensorID: String, value: Int, severity: Severity, isValid: Bool) {\n        self.sensorID = sensorID\n        self.value = value\n        self.severity = severity\n        self.isValid = isValid\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct CompactionReport: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let keptCount: Int\n    public let droppedCount: Int\n    public let droppedBySensor: [String: Int]\n\n    public init(keptCount: Int, droppedCount: Int, droppedBySensor: [String: Int]) {\n        self.keptCount = keptCount\n        self.droppedCount = droppedCount\n        self.droppedBySensor = droppedBySensor\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum BufferError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case nonPositiveRepeatLimit(Int)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct TelemetryBuffer: Sendable {\n    public private(set) var samples: [Sample]\n\n    public init(samples: [Sample]) {\n        self.samples = samples\n    }\n\n    // MARK: The shared compaction cursor\n\n    /// One read cursor sweeping every slot, one write cursor that only advances\n    /// when a sample is kept. Everything below `write` is final output;\n    /// everything at or above `read` is unexamined; the span between them is\n    /// the hole left by dropped samples, not a candidate answer.\n    ///\n    /// The keep test is handed the buffer as it stands, so it can look at what\n    /// has already been committed - that is the only reason Part 2 works\n    /// without a second array. It is also handed both cursors, because a keep\n    /// test that only sees the read cursor cannot express a repeat limit.\n    private mutating func compact(\n        keeping shouldKeep: (_ buffer: [Sample], _ read: Int, _ write: Int) -> Bool\n    ) -> CompactionReport {\n        var write = 0\n        var droppedBySensor: [String: Int] = [:]\n\n        for read in 0..<samples.count {\n            if shouldKeep(samples, read, write) {\n                samples[write] = samples[read]\n                write += 1\n            } else {\n                droppedBySensor[samples[read].sensorID, default: 0] += 1\n            }\n        }\n\n        let droppedCount = samples.count - write\n        samples.removeLast(droppedCount)\n        return CompactionReport(\n            keptCount: write,\n            droppedCount: droppedCount,\n            droppedBySensor: droppedBySensor\n        )\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Drop invalid readings in place\n\n    /// Remove every reading the sensor flagged as bad, rewriting the buffer in\n    /// place and reporting what went.\n    ///\n    /// The report carries the per-sensor attribution rather than only a new\n    /// length, because Part 2 needs exactly that and a bare count would force\n    /// it to walk the buffer a second time to rebuild it.\n    @discardableResult\n    public mutating func dropInvalid() -> CompactionReport {\n        compact { buffer, read, _ in buffer[read].isValid }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Cap repeated readings per sensor\n\n    /// Keep at most `limit` samples from each run of identical readings.\n    ///\n    /// A run is a stretch of consecutive samples sharing a sensor and a value:\n    /// a sensor that reports the same number ten times in a row has said one\n    /// thing, and the uplink budget only pays for `limit` of them.\n    ///\n    /// The keep test compares against the sample committed `limit` slots back,\n    /// not against the previous read. Comparing reads keeps one sample per run\n    /// whatever the limit says.\n    @discardableResult\n    public mutating func capRepeats(perSensor limit: Int) throws(BufferError) -> CompactionReport {\n        guard limit > 0 else { throw .nonPositiveRepeatLimit(limit) }\n\n        return compact { buffer, read, write in\n            guard write >= limit else { return true }\n            let committed = buffer[write - limit]\n            return committed.sensorID != buffer[read].sensorID || committed.value != buffer[read].value\n        }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Partition by severity in one pass\n\n    /// Reorder the buffer so every informational sample comes first, then every\n    /// warning, then every critical one, and report the range each band\n    /// occupies.\n    ///\n    /// This is the same invariant with three regions instead of two, so the\n    /// keep-or-drop cursor has nothing to decide and `compact` does not apply.\n    /// A low cursor marks the end of the settled informational band, a high\n    /// cursor marks the start of the settled critical band, and the read cursor\n    /// walks the unclassified middle.\n    ///\n    /// After a swap with the high cursor the read cursor must stay where it is:\n    /// the sample that arrived from the tail has not been looked at yet.\n    ///\n    /// Every severity appears in the result, with an empty range when the\n    /// buffer holds none of that band.\n    public mutating func partitionBySeverity() -> [Severity: Range<Int>] {\n        var low = 0\n        var read = 0\n        var high = samples.count - 1\n\n        while read <= high {\n            switch samples[read].severity {\n            case .info:\n                samples.swapAt(low, read)\n                low += 1\n                read += 1\n            case .warning:\n                read += 1\n            case .critical:\n                samples.swapAt(read, high)\n                high -= 1\n            }\n        }\n\n        return [\n            .info: 0..<low,\n            .warning: low..<read,\n            .critical: read..<samples.count,\n        ]\n    }\n}\n"};
